{
  "project_name": "prm_crosscoder_mechinterp",
  "source": "manual_revision_r5",
  "change_type": "major",
  "title": "Mechanistic Dissection of Process Reward Models: Three-Model Crosscoder Analysis with Falsification-Validated Feature Taxonomy and Exploratory Steering",
  "description": "We present the first crosscoder-based mechanistic analysis of Process Reward Model (PRM) paradigm differences, producing a falsification-validated feature taxonomy of verification mechanisms. Using Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct as a shared base, we compare three PRM arms: (A) the implicit PRM (base model as verifier), (B) a discriminative PRM (Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B), and (C) a generative PRM (ThinkPRM-adapted with chain-of-thought verification). We adopt the three-model sparse crosscoder architecture from Shi et al. (2604.25011), which jointly encodes residual-stream activations from all three models into a shared sparse feature space via model-specific encoder weights and shared decoder weights. This resolves the index misalignment problem inherent in pairwise crosscoder designs, where features identified in separate pairwise crosscoders are not directly comparable. Model Attribution Scores (MAS) provide continuous [0,1] attribution per model per feature, replacing the binary shared/exclusive classification of Dual-K approaches. We train one three-model crosscoder per layer at five representative depths (layers 4, 10, 16, 22, 28 of the 32-layer model), enhanced with BatchTopK activation (strictly superior to standard TopK per the Delta-Crosscoder literature). Our central hypotheses are decomposed into falsifiable sub-predictions: H1a predicts discriminative PRMs develop error-pattern features (detecting common mistakes like arithmetic or sign errors) but under-develop error-explanation features (multi-step logical chain verification); H1b predicts generative PRMs develop paradigm-exclusive error-explanation features (high MAS_C); H1c predicts the base model already encodes substantial error-detection via shared features, explaining why RL induces PRM capabilities. Explicit falsification criteria are specified: if discriminative PRMs show equally rich error-explanation features, the gap is inference-architecture-driven rather than representational; if generative PRMs show primarily fluency features, CoT verification is surface mimicry. We validate the feature taxonomy through behavioral falsification on PRM-Bench and ProcessBench, and conduct exploratory feature steering experiments (feature zeroing and amplification) to test whether identified features causally influence verification accuracy. Total compute is approximately 850 GPU-hours on A100-80GB. This work is differentiated from Shi et al. (2604.25011), who study RL/SFT generalization mechanisms, by its focus on PRM verification quality, the error-pattern vs. error-explanation taxonomy, falsification validation, and feature steering -- none of which are addressed in their work.",
  "core_problem": "Process Reward Models (PRMs) for mathematical reasoning exist in two paradigms -- discriminative (trained to classify step correctness) and generative (trained to produce chain-of-thought verification) -- yet their internal verification mechanisms remain opaque. It is unknown whether these paradigms develop fundamentally different representational strategies for error detection, or whether one paradigm merely performs surface-level mimicry of verification. Existing behavioral evaluations (PRM-Bench, ProcessBench) reveal performance differences but cannot explain WHY they arise at the mechanistic level. Without understanding the internal feature structure, practitioners cannot make principled choices between paradigms, predict failure modes, or design targeted improvements.",
  "opportunity": "Three developments converge to make this analysis newly tractable. First, the three-model sparse crosscoder (Shi et al., 2604.25011, April 2026) provides a validated architecture for jointly analyzing base, SFT, and RL models on the exact model family we study (Qwen2.5-7B), with code available at github.com/danshi777/RL-generalization. Their architecture solves the index misalignment problem that plagued pairwise crosscoder designs. Second, the Qwen2.5-Math ecosystem provides all three PRM arms from a shared base model (Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct), eliminating confounds from different pretraining corpora. Third, SAE-based feature analysis has been validated for fine-tuning analysis: Cheng et al. (2603.02908, ICLR 2026) demonstrate that SAE features predict cross-domain transferability on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct with Pearson correlation >0.7, confirming the informativeness of sparse features for understanding fine-tuning effects. The Delta-Crosscoder framework provides BatchTopK activation, which is strictly better than the standard TopK used in 2604.25011, offering an immediate architectural improvement.",
  "landscape": "Crosscoders (Lindsey et al., 2024) were introduced to find shared and model-exclusive features across related models. Delta-Crosscoders (Dunefsky et al., 2025) refined the architecture with BatchTopK activation functions and the Dual-K (20%/80%) design parameter for separating shared from exclusive features. Shi et al. (2604.25011, April 2026) extended crosscoders to a three-model setting (base, SFT, RL on Qwen2.5-7B), introducing Model Attribution Scores (MAS) as continuous feature attribution and finding that SFT creates specialized features while RL preserves base representations. However, their work studies RL/SFT generalization -- not PRM verification mechanisms, not error-pattern vs. error-explanation feature taxonomies, and not falsification validation. Separately, PRM evaluation benchmarks (PRM-Bench by Song et al., 2025; ProcessBench by Zheng et al., 2024) provide behavioral assessments but no mechanistic explanation. ThinkPRM (Khalifa et al., 2025) demonstrates that generative verification via chain-of-thought improves PRM performance, but the representational basis of this improvement is unknown. SAE-based cross-domain transferability prediction (Cheng et al., 2603.02908, ICLR 2026) validates that sparse features are informative for fine-tuning analysis on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct. No prior work has applied crosscoder-based mechanistic analysis to PRM paradigm comparison, constructed a falsification-validated feature taxonomy for verification mechanisms, or tested whether crosscoder-identified features causally influence PRM verification through steering.",
  "inspired_by": [
    "Shi et al. (2604.25011) -- Three-model sparse crosscoder on Qwen2.5-7B comparing base/SFT/RL models with Model Attribution Scores",
    "Lindsey et al. (2024) -- Original crosscoder architecture for comparing model pairs",
    "Dunefsky et al. (2025) -- Delta-Crosscoder with BatchTopK and Dual-K design for shared/exclusive feature separation",
    "Cheng et al. (2603.02908, ICLR 2026) -- SAE features predict cross-domain transferability on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (Pearson >0.7)",
    "Khalifa et al. (2025) -- ThinkPRM: generative chain-of-thought verification for PRMs",
    "Song et al. (2025) -- PRM-Bench: comprehensive PRM behavioral evaluation",
    "Zheng et al. (2024) -- ProcessBench: step-level process reward model evaluation"
  ],
  "approach": {
    "overview": "A three-phase study: (1) train three-model crosscoders to extract shared and paradigm-exclusive features across implicit, discriminative, and generative PRMs; (2) construct a falsification-validated feature taxonomy distinguishing error-pattern from error-explanation features; (3) conduct exploratory feature steering to test causal influence of identified features on verification accuracy.",
    "models_under_study": {
      "arm_A_implicit": "Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct (base model used as verifier without PRM-specific training)",
      "arm_B_discriminative": "Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B (discriminative PRM trained to classify step correctness)",
      "arm_C_generative": "Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct fine-tuned with ThinkPRM-style chain-of-thought verification training",
      "rationale": "All three arms share the same Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct base, eliminating pretraining corpus confounds and ensuring that feature differences are attributable to PRM training paradigm differences."
    },
    "phase_1_crosscoder_training": {
      "title": "Three-Model Crosscoder Training (Weeks 1-4)",
      "architecture": "Adopt the three-model sparse crosscoder from Shi et al. (2604.25011). The encoder uses model-specific weights with a shared bias: f(x_j) = ReLU(sum_{i in {A,B,C}} W_enc^(i) * a^(i)(x_j) + b_enc), where a^(i)(x_j) is the residual-stream activation at position j from model i. The decoder is shared across models with model-specific output: a_hat^(i)(x_j) = sum_k f_k(x_j) * W_dec,k^(i). This produces a single shared sparse feature space where each feature has a continuous attribution to each model.",
      "enhancement_over_2604_25011": "We replace standard TopK activation (used in Shi et al.) with BatchTopK from the Delta-Crosscoder framework, which dynamically adjusts sparsity across the batch and is strictly superior to fixed TopK in reconstruction quality per unit sparsity. This is a direct methodological improvement over 2604.25011.",
      "feature_attribution": "Model Attribution Score (MAS): MAS_i(k) = ||W_dec,k^(i)||_1 / sum_j ||W_dec,k^(j)||_1, yielding a continuous [0,1] score per model per feature. Features with MAS_B > 0.6 are discriminative-dominant; MAS_C > 0.6 are generative-dominant; max(MAS) < 0.5 are shared. This replaces the binary Dual-K classification, providing finer-grained attribution without arbitrary thresholds.",
      "layers": "Five representative layers of the 32-layer Qwen2.5-Math-7B architecture: layers 4 (early/syntactic), 10 (mid-early), 16 (middle/transitional), 22 (mid-late/semantic), 28 (late/decision). This extends Shi et al.'s single-layer design to capture the depth-dependent structure of verification features.",
      "feature_dictionary_size": "32,768 features per crosscoder (matching Shi et al.), yielding 5 crosscoders x 32K features = 163,840 total features before filtering.",
      "training_data": "Residual-stream activations collected from all three models processing identical mathematical reasoning traces from the MATH and GSM8K training sets, including both correct and erroneous solution steps. Approximately 500K token positions per layer.",
      "training_procedure": "Adam optimizer, learning rate 3e-4 with cosine decay, batch size 4096 tokens, 50K steps per crosscoder. L1 sparsity coefficient tuned to achieve ~95% dead feature rate (standard for interpretability-grade dictionaries). Each crosscoder trains in approximately 30 hours on 1x A100-80GB.",
      "compute": "5 crosscoders x 30h = 150 GPU-hours"
    },
    "phase_2_feature_taxonomy_and_falsification": {
      "title": "Feature Taxonomy Construction and Falsification Validation (Weeks 4-7)",
      "step_2a_automated_feature_labeling": "Apply automated interpretability (max-activating examples, activation patching) to label the top-500 most active features per crosscoder. Classify features into: (i) error-pattern features -- those that activate on common mathematical mistakes (arithmetic errors, sign errors, off-by-one errors, unit conversion errors) detectable by pattern matching without deep reasoning; (ii) error-explanation features -- those that activate on multi-step logical chain verification, constraint propagation, or proof-structure validation requiring extended reasoning; (iii) fluency features -- those that activate on surface-level formatting, notation, or presentation quality; (iv) shared mathematical features -- those with low MAS variance that encode general mathematical knowledge present in all three models.",
      "step_2b_hypothesis_testing": {
        "H1a": {
          "prediction": "Discriminative PRM training (Arm B) develops error-pattern features (high MAS_B, activating on common mistake types) but under-develops error-explanation features compared to generative PRM (Arm C).",
          "metric": "Count features classified as error-pattern with MAS_B > 0.6 vs. features classified as error-explanation with MAS_B > 0.6. Compare ratio to Arm C.",
          "expected_outcome": "Discriminative PRM has >= 2x more error-pattern features than error-explanation features; generative PRM has roughly balanced or error-explanation-dominant distribution."
        },
        "H1b": {
          "prediction": "Generative PRM training develops error-explanation features that are paradigm-exclusive (MAS_C > 0.7), representing novel verification capabilities not present in the base model or discriminative PRM.",
          "metric": "Count features classified as error-explanation with MAS_C > 0.7. These should constitute at least 15% of all generative-dominant features.",
          "expected_outcome": "A substantial cluster of generative-exclusive error-explanation features exists, concentrated in layers 16-28."
        },
        "H1c": {
          "prediction": "The base model (Arm A) already encodes substantial error-detection capability via shared features (max MAS < 0.5), explaining why RL induces PRM capabilities without explicit verification training.",
          "metric": "Fraction of error-pattern features that are shared (max MAS < 0.5) vs. paradigm-exclusive.",
          "expected_outcome": "At least 40% of error-pattern features are shared across all three models, with the base model contributing meaningfully to their activation."
        },
        "H2_falsification_rates": {
          "prediction": "Behavioral falsification on PRM-Bench and ProcessBench will confirm the feature taxonomy: features labeled as error-explanation should predict performance on multi-step verification tasks better than error-pattern features.",
          "metric": "Correlation between per-problem feature activation profiles and PRM-Bench/ProcessBench accuracy, stratified by feature class.",
          "falsification_criterion": "If error-pattern features predict multi-step verification accuracy equally well as error-explanation features (correlation difference < 0.05), the taxonomy distinction is not behaviorally meaningful."
        }
      },
      "step_2c_falsification_protocol": {
        "falsification_of_H1": "If discriminative PRMs show equally rich error-explanation features (comparable count and MAS values to generative PRM), then the performance gap is inference-architecture-driven (the discriminative model HAS the features but cannot leverage them in its classification head), not representational. This would be a significant finding redirecting the field toward inference-time interventions rather than training paradigm changes.",
        "falsification_of_CoT_value": "If generative PRMs show primarily fluency features (majority of MAS_C > 0.6 features classified as fluency rather than error-explanation), then CoT verification is surface mimicry -- the model learns to produce verification-like text without developing genuine multi-step reasoning features. This would challenge the ThinkPRM premise.",
        "validation_method": "Behavioral validation on PRM-Bench (comprehensive PRM evaluation) and ProcessBench (step-level evaluation). Features identified as causally relevant via activation patching should correlate with per-problem accuracy (Pearson r > 0.3 to be considered meaningful)."
      },
      "compute": "Feature analysis and falsification validation: ~200 GPU-hours (activation collection, patching experiments, benchmark evaluation)"
    },
    "phase_3_exploratory_steering": {
      "title": "Exploratory Feature Steering (Weeks 7-9)",
      "rationale": "Shi et al. (2604.25011) demonstrate that crosscoder features can be zeroed or amplified for causal intervention. We extend this to test whether identified PRM features causally influence verification accuracy.",
      "steering_experiments": [
        "Zero error-pattern features in discriminative PRM and measure accuracy drop on pattern-detectable errors vs. reasoning-requiring errors (expect selective degradation on pattern-detectable errors)",
        "Amplify error-explanation features in discriminative PRM and test whether accuracy on multi-step verification tasks improves (expect modest improvement if features are present but underutilized)",
        "Zero generative-exclusive error-explanation features in generative PRM and measure whether multi-step verification degrades while single-step accuracy is preserved",
        "Transfer: amplify base-model shared error-pattern features in a model without PRM training and test whether basic error detection emerges"
      ],
      "H3_prediction": "Feature steering produces selective, predictable accuracy changes: zeroing error-pattern features degrades pattern-matching accuracy by >= 10% without affecting reasoning accuracy; zeroing error-explanation features degrades reasoning accuracy by >= 10% without affecting pattern-matching accuracy.",
      "H3_falsification": "If steering produces uniform accuracy changes (both error types affected equally) or no significant changes, features are not causally specialized for their labeled function -- the taxonomy is correlational, not causal.",
      "compute": "Steering experiments and evaluation: ~150 GPU-hours"
    },
    "phase_4_aggregation_analysis": {
      "title": "Aggregation and Last-Step Scoring Analysis (Weeks 8-9, parallel with Phase 3)",
      "H4_prediction": "Feature activation patterns differ systematically between intermediate steps and final steps in mathematical solutions. Discriminative PRMs show stronger last-step features (consistent with last-step scoring bias documented in PRM literature); generative PRMs distribute verification features more uniformly across steps.",
      "method": "Compare MAS distributions and feature activation magnitudes at intermediate vs. final solution steps across the three arms. Use the three-model crosscoder features to identify step-position-dependent verification patterns.",
      "compute": "~50 GPU-hours (activation analysis on existing crosscoder features)"
    },
    "ablation_study": {
      "title": "FAST-SAE Ablation Comparison (Week 9-10)",
      "purpose": "Compare three-model crosscoder features against independently trained per-model SAEs to validate that the crosscoder's shared feature space provides genuine insight beyond what separate SAEs reveal.",
      "method": "Train FAST (Filtering-based Alignment of Sparse Transformers) SAEs on 2 layers (16, 28) for each of the 3 models. Compare feature overlap statistics, interpretability scores, and behavioral prediction accuracy.",
      "compute": "2 layers x 3 models x ~7h = ~40 GPU-hours (reduced from 3 layers in previous revision)"
    },
    "total_timeline": "10 weeks (Weeks 1-4: crosscoder training; Weeks 4-7: taxonomy and falsification; Weeks 7-9: steering; Weeks 8-10: aggregation analysis, ablation, paper writing)",
    "total_compute": "Approximately 850 GPU-hours on A100-80GB (150h crosscoder training + 200h taxonomy/falsification + 150h steering + 50h aggregation + 40h ablation + 110h data collection and miscellaneous + 150h contingency/reruns)",
    "novelty_claim": "This is the first crosscoder-based mechanistic analysis of PRM paradigm differences with a falsification-validated feature taxonomy. We are NOT claiming to be the first to use crosscoders for model comparison (Lindsey et al. 2024, Shi et al. 2604.25011). Our novelty is the specific application to PRM verification mechanisms, the error-pattern vs. error-explanation taxonomy, the explicit falsification protocol, and the causal validation via feature steering -- none of which appear in prior crosscoder work. Shi et al. (2604.25011) study why RL generalizes; we study how PRMs verify."
  },
  "resource_estimate": {
    "manual": {
      "team": "1-2 researchers",
      "timeline": "10-12 weeks",
      "gpu_hours": "~850h A100-80GB (crosscoder training: 150h; taxonomy/falsification: 200h; steering: 150h; aggregation: 50h; FAST-SAE ablation: 40h; data collection: 110h; contingency: 150h)",
      "estimated_gpu_cost": "$2,550-3,400 (A100-80GB at $3.00-4.00/hr)",
      "gpu_utilization": "~45-55% effective utilization due to researcher iteration cycles and analysis time"
    },
    "human_in_loop": {
      "team": "1 researcher, ~25min/day reviewing agent proposals and feature taxonomy labels",
      "timeline": "7-9 weeks",
      "gpu_hours": "~850h A100-80GB (same compute, faster wall-clock via automated experiment scheduling)",
      "estimated_gpu_cost": "$2,550-3,400 (A100-80GB at $3.00-4.00/hr)",
      "claude_api_cost": "$80-130 (~800-1300 turns Opus)",
      "gpu_utilization": "~65-75%; agent queues experiments promptly but pauses at taxonomy approval gates",
      "risk_note": "Agent may over-explore feature taxonomy alternatives; approval gates on taxonomy definitions mitigate waste"
    },
    "auto_research": {
      "team": "0 researchers after ~3h initial setup (model access, data pipeline configuration)",
      "timeline": "3-4 weeks with 2x A100-80GB",
      "gpu_hours": "~1100h A100-80GB (base 850h + agent-driven additional ablations on MAS thresholds, BatchTopK vs TopK comparison, layer selection validation, feature dictionary size sweep)",
      "estimated_gpu_cost": "$3,300-4,400 (A100-80GB at $3.00-4.00/hr)",
      "claude_api_cost": "$180-280 (~1,800-2,800 turns Opus)",
      "gpu_utilization": "~85-90%; agent queues experiments continuously",
      "speed_bottleneck": "Crosscoder training is the sequential bottleneck (5 layers, each 30h); taxonomy construction requires sequential human-interpretable analysis",
      "risk_note": "Exceeds base 850h budget but produces more thorough paper with additional ablations and robustness checks"
    }
  },
  "target_venues": [
    "ICML 2027 (expected deadline ~Jan 2027; top ML venue, strong fit for mechanistic interpretability + methodology contribution)",
    "ICLR 2027 (expected deadline ~Sep-Oct 2026; primary target if results ready by then, excellent fit for interpretability work)",
    "NeurIPS 2027 (expected deadline ~May 2027; backup target, allows comprehensive evaluation and ablation)",
    "EMNLP 2027 (expected deadline ~Jun 2027; if results emphasize NLP/reasoning aspects over pure interpretability)"
  ],
  "level": "PhD-level research project (mechanistic interpretability + mathematical reasoning + PRM evaluation)",
  "key_references": [
    {
      "citation": "Shi et al., 'Why Does Reinforcement Learning Generalize? A Mechanistic Analysis of Three-Model Sparse Crosscoders', arXiv 2604.25011, April 2026",
      "annotation": "PRIMARY METHODOLOGICAL FOUNDATION. Introduces the three-model sparse crosscoder on Qwen2.5-7B (base/SFT/RL) with Model Attribution Scores (MAS). Encoder: f(x_j) = ReLU(sum W_enc^(i) a^(i) + b_enc), MAS_i = ||W_dec,k^(i)||_1 / sum_j||W_dec,k^(j)||_1. Uses standard TopK (we upgrade to BatchTopK), single middle layer (we extend to 5 layers), 32K features. Finds SFT creates specialized features, RL preserves base representations. Does NOT study PRMs, verification quality, error-pattern vs. error-explanation distinction, falsification validation, or feature steering. Code: github.com/danshi777/RL-generalization. Their three-model approach solves the index misalignment problem we had with pairwise crosscoders."
    },
    {
      "citation": "Lindsey et al., 'Crosscoders', Anthropic Research, 2024",
      "annotation": "Introduced the crosscoder architecture for finding shared and model-exclusive features across related models. Foundation for all subsequent crosscoder work including Delta-Crosscoders and three-model crosscoders."
    },
    {
      "citation": "Dunefsky et al., 'Delta-Crosscoders: Improving Sparse Autoencoders for Comparative Model Analysis', 2025",
      "annotation": "Refined crosscoder architecture with BatchTopK activation (strictly superior to TopK) and Dual-K 20%/80% design parameter. We adopt BatchTopK as an improvement over Shi et al.'s TopK. Dual-K becomes inapplicable in the three-model setting where MAS provides continuous attribution."
    },
    {
      "citation": "Cheng et al., 'SAE as a Crystal Ball: Predicting Cross-Domain Transferability with Sparse Autoencoder Features', arXiv 2603.02908, ICLR 2026",
      "annotation": "Validates SAE feature informativeness for fine-tuning analysis. Tests on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, achieves Pearson >0.7 for predicting cross-domain transferability from SAE features. Supports our premise that sparse features carry meaningful signal about fine-tuning-induced representational changes."
    },
    {
      "citation": "Khalifa et al., 'ThinkPRM: Thinking Ahead for Process Reward Modeling', 2025",
      "annotation": "Demonstrates that generative chain-of-thought verification improves PRM performance over discriminative approaches. Provides the training methodology for our Arm C (generative PRM). Our work aims to mechanistically explain WHY generative verification works better."
    },
    {
      "citation": "Song et al., 'PRM-Bench: A Fine-grained and Challenging Benchmark for Process-Level Reward Models', 2025",
      "annotation": "Comprehensive PRM behavioral evaluation benchmark. Used for falsification validation of our feature taxonomy -- features labeled as causally relevant should correlate with per-problem accuracy."
    },
    {
      "citation": "Zheng et al., 'ProcessBench: Identifying Process Errors in Mathematical Reasoning', 2024",
      "annotation": "Step-level process reward model evaluation. Used alongside PRM-Bench for behavioral validation and for testing H4 (aggregation/last-step scoring analysis)."
    },
    {
      "citation": "Qwen Team, 'Qwen2.5-Math Technical Report: Toward Mathematical Expert Model via Self-Improvement', 2024",
      "annotation": "Provides the model family (Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B) used for Arms A and B. The shared base ensures fair comparison across PRM paradigms."
    },
    {
      "citation": "Bricken et al., 'Towards Monosemanticity: Decomposing Language Models With Dictionary Learning', Anthropic Research, 2023",
      "annotation": "Foundational work on sparse autoencoders for mechanistic interpretability. Establishes methodology for feature labeling via max-activating examples."
    },
    {
      "citation": "Templeton et al., 'Scaling Monosemanticity: Extracting Interpretable Features from Claude 3 Sonnet', Anthropic Research, 2024",
      "annotation": "Demonstrates SAE feature interpretability at scale. Establishes conventions for automated feature labeling and activation patching validation."
    },
    {
      "citation": "Lieberum et al., 'FAST (Filtering-based Alignment of Sparse Transformers) SAEs', 2024",
      "annotation": "Provides the per-model SAE methodology used in our ablation study comparing crosscoder features against independently trained SAEs."
    },
    {
      "citation": "Lightman et al., 'Let's Verify Step by Step', 2023",
      "annotation": "Foundational work on process reward models for mathematical reasoning. Establishes the discriminative PRM paradigm and step-level supervision approach."
    }
  ],
  "revision_notes": {
    "round_1_issues_addressed": [
      "Added explicit falsification criteria for all hypotheses",
      "Specified concrete feature taxonomy (error-pattern vs. error-explanation vs. fluency vs. shared mathematical)",
      "Included behavioral validation protocol on PRM-Bench and ProcessBench",
      "Added compute budget breakdown by phase"
    ],
    "round_2_issues_addressed": [
      "Strengthened novelty claim with explicit differentiation from prior crosscoder work",
      "Added H4 (aggregation/last-step scoring analysis)",
      "Specified target model family (Qwen2.5-Math) with rationale for shared-base design",
      "Added FAST-SAE ablation comparison"
    ],
    "round_3_issues_addressed": [
      "Expanded landscape section with comprehensive coverage of crosscoder, PRM, and SAE literatures",
      "Added specific layer selection rationale (5 layers covering syntactic-to-decision depth)",
      "Included feature dictionary size justification (32K matching Shi et al.)",
      "Added steering experiment details with expected effect sizes"
    ],
    "round_4_issues_addressed": [
      "Reviewer noted declining scores (3.8 to 3.5); this round is a major revision",
      "The four issues from round 4 reviewer feedback are addressed as follows"
    ],
    "round_5_major_changes": {
      "change_1_three_model_crosscoder": "ADOPTED three-model crosscoder from Shi et al. (2604.25011) replacing two pairwise Delta-Crosscoders. This is simpler (5 crosscoder runs instead of 10), more principled (single shared feature space, no index misalignment), and already validated on our exact model family (Qwen2.5-7B). MAS provides continuous [0,1] attribution replacing binary Dual-K classification. Enhanced with BatchTopK (from Delta-Crosscoder, strictly better than TopK used in 2604.25011).",
      "change_2_h1_tautology_fix": "Decomposed H1 into three falsifiable sub-predictions (H1a: error-pattern vs. error-explanation asymmetry in discriminative PRMs; H1b: generative-exclusive error-explanation features; H1c: base model shared error-detection). Added explicit falsification criteria: if discriminative shows equal error-explanation features, gap is inference-architecture-driven; if generative shows primarily fluency features, CoT is surface mimicry. This eliminates the tautological 'discriminative amplifies fluency, generative develops reasoning' framing.",
      "change_3_dual_k_to_mas": "Confirmed that Dual-K 20%/80% IS the actual Delta-Crosscoder design parameter, BUT it is inapplicable in the three-model crosscoder setting. MAS provides continuous attribution instead of binary shared/exclusive classification, which is more informative for three-way comparison.",
      "change_4_new_reference_2603_02908": "Added Cheng et al. (2603.02908, ICLR 2026) -- 'SAE as a Crystal Ball' validates SAE feature informativeness for fine-tuning analysis on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct with Pearson >0.7.",
      "change_5_novelty_reframing": "Reframed novelty claim from 'first crosscoder-based comparative mechanistic analysis' to 'first crosscoder-based mechanistic analysis of PRM paradigm differences with falsification-validated feature taxonomy'. Explicit differentiation from Shi et al.: they study RL/SFT generalization, we study PRM verification mechanisms.",
      "change_6_methodology_simplification": "Phase 1 simplified to one three-model crosscoder per layer (5 runs, down from 10 for two pairwise). FAST-SAE reduced to ablation comparison (2 layers instead of 3). Crosscoder steering leverages 2604.25011's demonstrated feature zeroing/amplification capability.",
      "change_7_compute_reduction": "Total reduced from ~950h to ~850h: crosscoder training 150h (was 200h), FAST-SAE ablation 40h (was 60h)."
    }
  }
}

{
  "project_name": "prm_crosscoder_mechinterp",
  "source": "manual_revision_r5",
  "change_type": "major",
  "title": "Mechanistic Dissection of Process Reward Models: Three-Model Crosscoder Analysis with Falsification-Validated Feature Taxonomy and Exploratory Steering",
  "description": "We present the first crosscoder-based mechanistic analysis of Process Reward Model (PRM) paradigm differences, producing a falsification-validated feature taxonomy of verification mechanisms. Using Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct as a shared base, we compare three PRM arms: (A) the implicit PRM (base model as verifier), (B) a discriminative PRM (Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B), and (C) a generative PRM (ThinkPRM-adapted with chain-of-thought verification). We adopt the three-model sparse crosscoder architecture from Shi et al. (2604.25011), which jointly encodes residual-stream activations from all three models into a shared sparse feature space via model-specific encoder weights and shared decoder weights. This resolves the index misalignment problem inherent in pairwise crosscoder designs, where features identified in separate pairwise crosscoders are not directly comparable. Model Attribution Scores (MAS) provide continuous [0,1] attribution per model per feature, replacing the binary shared/exclusive classification of Dual-K approaches. We train one three-model crosscoder per layer at five representative depths (layers 4, 10, 16, 22, 28 of the 32-layer model), enhanced with BatchTopK activation (strictly superior to standard TopK per the Delta-Crosscoder literature). Our central hypotheses are decomposed into falsifiable sub-predictions: H1a predicts discriminative PRMs develop error-pattern features (detecting common mistakes like arithmetic or sign errors) but under-develop error-explanation features (multi-step logical chain verification); H1b predicts generative PRMs develop paradigm-exclusive error-explanation features (high MAS_C); H1c predicts the base model already encodes substantial error-detection via shared features, explaining why RL induces PRM capabilities. Explicit falsification criteria are specified: if discriminative PRMs show equally rich error-explanation features, the gap is inference-architecture-driven rather than representational; if generative PRMs show primarily fluency features, CoT verification is surface mimicry. We validate the feature taxonomy through behavioral falsification on PRM-Bench and ProcessBench, and conduct exploratory feature steering experiments (feature zeroing and amplification) to test whether identified features causally influence verification accuracy. Total compute is approximately 850 GPU-hours on A100-80GB. This work is differentiated from Shi et al. (2604.25011), who study RL/SFT generalization mechanisms, by its focus on PRM verification quality, the error-pattern vs. error-explanation taxonomy, falsification validation, and feature steering -- none of which are addressed in their work.",
  "core_problem": "Process Reward Models (PRMs) for mathematical reasoning exist in two paradigms -- discriminative (trained to classify step correctness) and generative (trained to produce chain-of-thought verification) -- yet their internal verification mechanisms remain opaque. It is unknown whether these paradigms develop fundamentally different representational strategies for error detection, or whether one paradigm merely performs surface-level mimicry of verification. Existing behavioral evaluations (PRM-Bench, ProcessBench) reveal performance differences but cannot explain WHY they arise at the mechanistic level. Without understanding the internal feature structure, practitioners cannot make principled choices between paradigms, predict failure modes, or design targeted improvements.",
  "opportunity": "Three developments converge to make this analysis newly tractable. First, the three-model sparse crosscoder (Shi et al., 2604.25011, April 2026) provides a validated architecture for jointly analyzing base, SFT, and RL models on the exact model family we study (Qwen2.5-7B), with code available at github.com/danshi777/RL-generalization. Their architecture solves the index misalignment problem that plagued pairwise crosscoder designs. Second, the Qwen2.5-Math ecosystem provides all three PRM arms from a shared base model (Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct), eliminating confounds from different pretraining corpora. Third, SAE-based feature analysis has been validated for fine-tuning analysis: Cheng et al. (2603.02908, ICLR 2026) demonstrate that SAE features predict cross-domain transferability on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct with Pearson correlation >0.7, confirming the informativeness of sparse features for understanding fine-tuning effects. The Delta-Crosscoder framework provides BatchTopK activation, which is strictly better than the standard TopK used in 2604.25011, offering an immediate architectural improvement.",
  "landscape": "Crosscoders (Lindsey et al., 2024) were introduced to find shared and model-exclusive features across related models. Delta-Crosscoders (Dunefsky et al., 2025) refined the architecture with BatchTopK activation functions and the Dual-K (20%/80%) design parameter for separating shared from exclusive features. Shi et al. (2604.25011, April 2026) extended crosscoders to a three-model setting (base, SFT, RL on Qwen2.5-7B), introducing Model Attribution Scores (MAS) as continuous feature attribution and finding that SFT creates specialized features while RL preserves base representations. However, their work studies RL/SFT generalization -- not PRM verification mechanisms, not error-pattern vs. error-explanation feature taxonomies, and not falsification validation. Separately, PRM evaluation benchmarks (PRM-Bench by Song et al., 2025; ProcessBench by Zheng et al., 2024) provide behavioral assessments but no mechanistic explanation. ThinkPRM (Khalifa et al., 2025) demonstrates that generative verification via chain-of-thought improves PRM performance, but the representational basis of this improvement is unknown. SAE-based cross-domain transferability prediction (Cheng et al., 2603.02908, ICLR 2026) validates that sparse features are informative for fine-tuning analysis on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct. No prior work has applied crosscoder-based mechanistic analysis to PRM paradigm comparison, constructed a falsification-validated feature taxonomy for verification mechanisms, or tested whether crosscoder-identified features causally influence PRM verification through steering.",
  "inspired_by": [
    "Shi et al. (2604.25011) -- Three-model sparse crosscoder on Qwen2.5-7B comparing base/SFT/RL models with Model Attribution Scores",
    "Lindsey et al. (2024) -- Original crosscoder architecture for comparing model pairs",
    "Dunefsky et al. (2025) -- Delta-Crosscoder with BatchTopK and Dual-K design for shared/exclusive feature separation",
    "Cheng et al. (2603.02908, ICLR 2026) -- SAE features predict cross-domain transferability on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (Pearson >0.7)",
    "Khalifa et al. (2025) -- ThinkPRM: generative chain-of-thought verification for PRMs",
    "Song et al. (2025) -- PRM-Bench: comprehensive PRM behavioral evaluation",
    "Zheng et al. (2024) -- ProcessBench: step-level process reward model evaluation"
  ],
  "approach": {
    "overview": "A three-phase study: (1) train three-model crosscoders to extract shared and paradigm-exclusive features across implicit, discriminative, and generative PRMs; (2) construct a falsification-validated feature taxonomy distinguishing error-pattern from error-explanation features; (3) conduct exploratory feature steering to test causal influence of identified features on verification accuracy.",
    "models_under_study": {
      "arm_A_implicit": "Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct (base model used as verifier without PRM-specific training)",
      "arm_B_discriminative": "Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B (discriminative PRM trained to classify step correctness)",
      "arm_C_generative": "Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct fine-tuned with ThinkPRM-style chain-of-thought verification training",
      "rationale": "All three arms share the same Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct base, eliminating pretraining corpus confounds and ensuring that feature differences are attributable to PRM training paradigm differences."
