  "title": "Mechanistic Dissection of Process Reward Models: Three-Model Crosscoder Analysis with Falsification-Validated Feature Taxonomy and Exploratory Steering",
  "description": "We present the first crosscoder-based mechanistic analysis of Process Reward Model (PRM) paradigm differences, producing a falsification-validated feature taxonomy of verification mechanisms. Using Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct as a shared base, we compare three PRM arms: (A) the implicit PRM (base model as verifier), (B) a discriminative PRM (Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B), and (C) a generative PRM (ThinkPRM-adapted with chain-of-thought verification). We adopt the three-model sparse crosscoder architecture from Shi et al. (2604.25011), which jointly encodes residual-stream activations from all three models into a shared sparse feature space via model-specific encoder weights and shared decoder weights. This resolves the index misalignment problem inherent in pairwise crosscoder designs, where features identified in separate pairwise crosscoders are not directly comparable. Model Attribution Scores (MAS) provide continuous [0,1] attribution per model per feature, replacing the binary shared/exclusive classification of Dual-K approaches. We train one three-model crosscoder per layer at five representative depths (layers 4, 10, 16, 22, 28 of the 32-layer model), enhanced with BatchTopK activation (strictly superior to standard TopK per the Delta-Crosscoder literature). Our central hypotheses are decomposed into falsifiable sub-predictions: H1a predicts discriminative PRMs develop error-pattern features (detecting common mistakes like arithmetic or sign errors) but under-develop error-explanation features (multi-step logical chain verification); H1b predicts generative PRMs develop paradigm-exclusive error-explanation features (high MAS_C); H1c predicts the base model already encodes substantial error-detection via shared features, explaining why RL induces PRM capabilities. Explicit falsification criteria are specified: if discriminative PRMs show equally rich error-explanation features, the gap is inference-architecture-driven rather than representational; if generative PRMs show primarily fluency features, CoT verification is surface mimicry. We validate the feature taxonomy through behavioral falsification on PRM-Bench and ProcessBench, and conduct exploratory feature steering experiments (feature zeroing and amplification) to test whether identified features causally influence verification accuracy. Total compute is approximately 850 GPU-hours on A100-80GB. This work is differentiated from Shi et al. (2604.25011), who study RL/SFT generalization mechanisms, by its focus on PRM verification quality, the error-pattern vs. error-explanation taxonomy, falsification validation, and feature steering -- none of which are addressed in their work.",
  "core_problem": "Process Reward Models (PRMs) for mathematical reasoning exist in two paradigms -- discriminative (trained to classify step correctness) and generative (trained to produce chain-of-thought verification) -- yet their internal verification mechanisms remain opaque. It is unknown whether these paradigms develop fundamentally different representational strategies for error detection, or whether one paradigm merely performs surface-level mimicry of verification. Existing behavioral evaluations (PRM-Bench, ProcessBench) reveal performance differences but cannot explain WHY they arise at the mechanistic level. Without understanding the internal feature structure, practitioners cannot make principled choices between paradigms, predict failure modes, or design targeted improvements.",
  "opportunity": "Three developments converge to make this analysis newly tractable. First, the three-model sparse crosscoder (Shi et al., 2604.25011, April 2026) provides a validated architecture for jointly analyzing base, SFT, and RL models on the exact model family we study (Qwen2.5-7B), with code available at github.com/danshi777/RL-generalization. Their architecture solves the index misalignment problem that plagued pairwise crosscoder designs. Second, the Qwen2.5-Math ecosystem provides all three PRM arms from a shared base model (Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct), eliminating confounds from different pretraining corpora. Third, SAE-based feature analysis has been validated for fine-tuning analysis: Cheng et al. (2603.02908, ICLR 2026) demonstrate that SAE features predict cross-domain transferability on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct with Pearson correlation >0.7, confirming the informativeness of sparse features for understanding fine-tuning effects. The Delta-Crosscoder framework provides BatchTopK activation, which is strictly better than the standard TopK used in 2604.25011, offering an immediate architectural improvement.",
  "landscape": "Crosscoders (Lindsey et al., 2024) were introduced to find shared and model-exclusive features across related models. Delta-Crosscoders (Dunefsky et al., 2025) refined the architecture with BatchTopK activation functions and the Dual-K (20%/80%) design parameter for separating shared from exclusive features. Shi et al. (2604.25011, April 2026) extended crosscoders to a three-model setting (base, SFT, RL on Qwen2.5-7B), introducing Model Attribution Scores (MAS) as continuous feature attribution and finding that SFT creates specialized features while RL preserves base representations. However, their work studies RL/SFT generalization -- not PRM verification mechanisms, not error-pattern vs. error-explanation feature taxonomies, and not falsification validation. Separately, PRM evaluation benchmarks (PRM-Bench by Song et al., 2025; ProcessBench by Zheng et al., 2024) provide behavioral assessments but no mechanistic explanation. ThinkPRM (Khalifa et al., 2025) demonstrates that generative verification via chain-of-thought improves PRM performance, but the representational basis of this improvement is unknown. SAE-based cross-domain transferability prediction (Cheng et al., 2603.02908, ICLR 2026) validates that sparse features are informative for fine-tuning analysis on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct. No prior work has applied crosscoder-based mechanistic analysis to PRM paradigm comparison, constructed a falsification-validated feature taxonomy for verification mechanisms, or tested whether crosscoder-identified features causally influence PRM verification through steering.",
  "inspired_by": [
    "Shi et al. (2604.25011) -- Three-model sparse crosscoder on Qwen2.5-7B comparing base/SFT/RL models with Model Attribution Scores",
    "Lindsey et al. (2024) -- Original crosscoder architecture for comparing model pairs",
    "Dunefsky et al. (2025) -- Delta-Crosscoder with BatchTopK and Dual-K design for shared/exclusive feature separation",
    "Cheng et al. (2603.02908, ICLR 2026) -- SAE features predict cross-domain transferability on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (Pearson >0.7)",
    "Khalifa et al. (2025) -- ThinkPRM: generative chain-of-thought verification for PRMs",
    "Song et al. (2025) -- PRM-Bench: comprehensive PRM behavioral evaluation",
    "Zheng et al. (2024) -- ProcessBench: step-level process reward model evaluation"
  ],
  "approach": {
    "overview": "A three-phase study: (1) train three-model crosscoders to extract shared and paradigm-exclusive features across implicit, discriminative, and generative PRMs; (2) construct a falsification-validated feature taxonomy distinguishing error-pattern from error-explanation features; (3) conduct exploratory feature steering to test causal influence of identified features on verification accuracy.",
    "models_under_study": {
      "arm_A_implicit": "Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct (base model used as verifier without PRM-specific training)",
      "arm_B_discriminative": "Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B (discriminative PRM trained to classify step correctness)",
      "arm_C_generative": "Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct fine-tuned with ThinkPRM-style chain-of-thought verification training",
      "rationale": "All three arms share the same Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct base, eliminating pretraining corpus confounds and ensuring that feature differences are attributable to PRM training paradigm differences."
